  "question": {
    "text": "Consider the quadratic equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#8800;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo></math> Let <math><mi>S</mi></math> be the set of all integral values of <math><mi>c</mi></math> for which one root of the equation lies in the interval <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> and its other root lies in the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;Then the number of elements in <math><mi>S</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "2oCcB",
      "text": "<math><mn>11</mn></math>",
